        contentType: 'TEXT',
        contentUrl: null,
        content: `LOADING PROTOCOLS, EXCAVATOR INTERACTION & PAYLOAD MANAGEMENT

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 1: ENTERING THE LOADING ZONE
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The loading zone (where the excavator or face shovel is operating) is a high-traffic, high-risk area. Clear communication and strict adherence to positioning protocols are mandatory.

Communication with the Loading Tool:
The excavator operator controls the loading zone. You must not enter the loading zone until you have established positive communication and the excavator operator has explicitly called you in (via radio, or via standard horn/light signals).
→ Wait at a safe distance (the staging area) until the truck ahead of you has cleared and the excavator operator signals you.

Spotting (Positioning the Truck):
The goal is to position the truck precisely where the excavator operator needs it, minimizing the swing angle for the excavator (which increases production efficiency).
1. The Setup: The excavator operator will usually position their bucket in the air to indicate where they want the centre of your tub to be.
2. The Approach: Drive in smoothly. Do not speed.
3. Reversing: Reversing an ADT can be tricky because of the articulation. Use your mirrors and reverse camera. Keep the truck as straight as possible. If the truck is bent (articulated) while loading, it is significantly less stable and more prone to tipping if loaded unevenly.
4. Stopping: Stop smoothly. The excavator operator will typically sound their horn once to tell you to stop.
5. Secure the Truck: Once in position, shift the transmission to Neutral and apply the Park Brake. DO NOT leave the truck in gear while being loaded. If your foot slips off the brake when 10 tonnes of rock is dropped into the tub, the truck will lurch forward.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 2: BEHAVIOUR DURING LOADING
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Getting loaded is a violent process. A large excavator might drop 10 to 15 tonnes of material into the tub with a single pass.

Operator Safety:
→ NEVER leave the cab while the truck is being loaded.
→ Keep your seatbelt securely fastened. The impact of the material dropping can bounce the operator out of the seat, causing head strikes against the roof.
→ Keep your hands off the steering wheel. If a large rock strikes the side of the tub off-centre, it can violently jerk the steering linkages. If you are holding the wheel, it will spin rapidly and can break your wrists or fingers.
→ Monitor the loading via your mirrors. Watch for rocks that might roll over the headboard towards the cab (cab-strikes).

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 3: PAYLOAD MANAGEMENT AND WEIGHT DISTRIBUTION
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

An ADT has a specific Maximum Payload Capacity (e.g., a Volvo A40G carries 39,000 kg). Overloading the truck is extremely dangerous.

The Dangers of Overloading:
1. Braking Failure: The brakes are designed to stop the rated gross vehicle weight. If you are 5 tonnes overloaded, the truck will not stop in time, and the retarder will not be able to hold the truck on a downgrade.
2. Structural Failure: Overloading bends the chassis, destroys wheel bearings, and blows out tyres.
3. Rollover Risk: An overloaded tub raises the Centre of Gravity, making the truck drastically more likely to tip over on corners or uneven ground.

Weight Distribution:
The load must be evenly distributed across the tub.
→ If the load is too far back (tail-heavy), it lifts the front wheels of the tractor off the ground. You will lose all steering control (the front wheels will just skim across the mud).
→ If the load is heavily biased to one side (e.g., the excavator dumped all the heavy boulders on the left side), the truck becomes laterally unstable and is highly likely to roll over on a cambered road.
→ The excavator operator should aim to place the first bucket load gently in the centre of the tub to act as a cushion, then build the load evenly.

Leaving the Loading Zone:
When the excavator operator has finished loading, they will signal you (usually two blasts of the horn).
1. Shift into Drive.
2. Release the park brake.
3. Move off smoothly and deliberately. Do not accelerate harshly, as this causes the material to shift backwards and spill off the tailgate, creating tyre hazards on the haul road for following vehicles.`
